{"kind":"assert","text":"Question on 4f697a7a for the cutover head: does a retained birth count in the producer's per-entry retained-byte bound?\n\nderive/src/field-retention.ts:39-47 fieldRetainedBytes charges min(facts, usesPerEntry) × entryBytes for an entry's foreign copies. holds.ts:409 uses that bound for producer capacity. Under 4f697a7a, a later use (seq above 0) from a foreign sequence the scope has not seen also keeps that sequence's first entry, as an entry row with a retained_birth association. The bound does not count that birth. One entry can therefore retain up to one more entryBytes for each new foreign sequence: in the worst case, twice the present bound.\n\nOptions:\n(a) Leave fieldRetainedBytes as it is. A birth is kept once per sequence and shared by later entries, so it sits outside the per-entry bound. The note states the worst case: up to min(facts, usesPerEntry) × entryBytes more per entry, and only on the first use of each sequence. This matches 4f697a7a, which keeps the v17 arithmetic, and 6d658a9f, which changes no cap or accounting.\n(b) Add one entryBytes for each possible new sequence, at most min(facts, usesPerEntry). The producer capacity at holds.ts:409 doubles its foreign term. Definitions admitted today, destination@17 among them, may then fail capacity.\n\nI recommend (a), with the worst case stated in the note. Option (b) changes admitted capacity, which 4f697a7a ruled out. If you want the extra bytes enforced, I recommend filing that as a follow-up beside ARC-1. The worker has made no change."}